screened.cheapest_test:
Two steps, both with assets we already have. Step 1 (free, about 30 s) measures how much of the expert set a 64-token and a 500-token prefill touch. Use the prompt's decode traces as a proxy, because no prefill trace exists. Command: cd /tmp/screen2-r7-0 && python3 -I -c "import sys,glob,re; sys.path.insert(0,'<tinyram-llm>/tools'); import collections; 
for f in sorted(glob.glob('<tinyram-llm>/data/traces/qwen3-30b_*.txt')):
  L=collections.defaultdict(list)
  for l in open(f):
    p=l.split()
    if len(p)>8: L[p[0]].append(set(map(int,p[1:9])))
  for n in (64,300):
    u=[len(set().union(*v[:n])) for v in L.values() if len(v)>=n]
    print(f.split('/')[-1],n,'tokens: unique experts per layer',sum(u)/len(u),'of 128')
". Expected: about 108-120 of 128 at 64 tokens if routing is near-uniform (about 100% at 300). Even decode traces, which are more clustered than prefill, should show 60-90%. Step 2 (needs host run): llama-bench -p 64 and -p 500 -n 0 on OLMoE at a cap. This measures the real time of block 1 vs the full prompt. Kill criterion: if the 64-token block takes more than 50% of the 500-token prefill time, or touches more than 50% of the experts, the idea is dead. Also dead if the first 64 prompt tokens do not predict the greedy answer, shown by comparing greedy text from a 64-token truncated prompt vs the full prompt on 20 prompts with exact-match under 70% of the first 16 tokens. The step 2 timing uses the existing llama-bench and OLMoE binary, so it is a few minutes of host time.
